:name: Duet 3 MB6HC on a real network
:description: Boots the board with its MAC bridged to a Linux TAP device, so the host can reach its HTTP server.
#
# This one only works inside the Lima guest. Renode needs a layer-2 TAP interface and macOS has none
# (utun is layer 3, and the third-party kexts do not load on Apple Silicon), which is why the VM exists.

include $tcmodel
include $piomodel
include $afecmodel
include $xdmacmodel
include $hsmcimodel
include $rstcmodel
include $usartspimodel

mach create "duet3_mb6hc"
machine LoadPlatformDescription $plat

# Renode re-runs the "reset" macro on every machine reset, so the boot vectors are
# re-applied when the firmware resets itself (M999 / DWC reboot). Setting VectorTableOffset
# once outside a macro does not survive: the core would restart on an empty ITCM.
macro reset
"""
    sysbus LoadBinary $fw 0x400000
    sysbus LoadSymbolsFrom $elf
    cpu VectorTableOffset 0x400000
    cpu PC `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x400004`
    cpu SP `sysbus ReadDoubleWord 0x400000`
"""
runMacro $reset

# The TAP is not something the MAC attaches to directly. connector Connect takes
# (IEmulationElement, IConnectable), and the switch is the IConnectable hub - so the MAC and the host
# TAP each connect to the switch, and the switch bridges them.
# A real, writable FAT32 card. persistent=true so DWC uploads and edits survive a restart.
machine SdCardFromFile $sd hsmci 0x4000000 true "sd"

emulation CreateSwitch "switch"
connector Connect gem switch
# The TAP registers under the "host." namespace, not under the bare name given to CreateTap - so
# "connector Connect tap switch" fails with a signature mismatch that looks like a type problem but
# is really a name-resolution one. Renode's own scripts/single-node/nucleo_h753zi_hubris.resc shows
# the working form.
# Not persistent: setup_guest.sh already created tap0 and gave it 192.168.100.1/24, and asking Renode
# to create a persistent one replaces the interface and discards that address.
emulation CreateTap "tap0" "tap"
connector Connect host.tap switch

# Aux2 on USART2 stays available as a console, so G-code can still be driven while the network is up.
emulation CreateServerSocketTerminal 3456 "aux2" false
connector Connect sysbus.usart2 aux2

# Errors only. This instance runs for hours, and at warning level the SVD-stubbed peripherals
# produce enough output to fill a filesystem - which is exactly what happened.
logLevel 3
start
